<main id="main"><header class="hero"><div class="shell"><h1>Supply Chain and Fulfilment</h1><p class="lede">Where does the delivery date you committed to actually break?</p><div class="bound"><div class="end from"><b>Demand signal</b><span>The commitment is created</span></div><div class="span"></div><div class="end to"><b>Delivery confirmed</b><span>The commitment is met</span></div></div></div></header><section class="band"><div class="shell band-grid"><div class="band-rail"><h2>Why this process resists visibility</h2></div><div class="band-body"><p class="intro">Fulfilment is reported as a percentage and experienced as a series of recoveries that largely go unrecorded.</p><ul class="gaps"><li><h3>On-time delivery reports the verdict</h3><p>It tells you the date was missed. It cannot tell you whether the date was lost in planning, at a supplier, on the warehouse floor or at the carrier.</p></li><li><h3>The chain crosses companies as well as systems</h3><p>Suppliers, contract manufacturers, third-party logistics providers and carriers each hold part of the record, and much of it arrives late or in a form that is hard to reconcile.</p></li><li><h3>Plans overwrite their own history</h3><p>Each replan replaces the last, so the original promise and the moment it became undeliverable are often lost from the system that made it.</p></li><li><h3>Expediting is an invisible subsidy</h3><p>The calls, swaps and premium freight that rescue a date leave little trace, so a process that depends on rescue can look like a process that works.</p></li><li><h3>Site variation is defended more often than measured</h3><p>Most locations treat their route as a necessary adaptation, and without comparable execution data the claim is difficult to test.</p></li></ul></div></div></section><section class="where"><div class="shell"><div class="where-head"><h2>Where time and effort accumulate</h2><p>The process is normally drawn as six clean stages. Execution data shows what actually collects at each one, and how much of the cycle is spent waiting rather than working.</p></div><div class="spine"><div aria-hidden="true" class="spine-rail"></div><ol class="stages"><li class="stage"><span aria-hidden="true" class="dot"></span><span class="n">Stage 1</span><h3>Demand and planning</h3><ul class="frict"><li>Signals arriving late or unstructured</li><li>Plans re-run after the commitment is made</li><li>Forecasts overridden manually</li><li>Safety stock adjusted case by case</li></ul></li><li class="stage"><span aria-hidden="true" class="dot"></span><span class="n">Stage 2</span><h3>Supply and procurement</h3><ul class="frict"><li>Supplier confirmations chased by hand</li><li>Lead times that no longer reflect reality</li><li>Substitutions approved outside the plan</li><li>Shortages surfacing at the line</li></ul></li><li class="stage"><span aria-hidden="true" class="dot"></span><span class="n">Stage 3</span><h3>Production and inbound</h3><ul class="frict"><li>Schedule changes after release</li><li>Quality holds and rework loops</li><li>Unplanned changeovers</li><li>Receipts posted after the goods arrive</li></ul></li><li class="stage"><span aria-hidden="true" class="dot"></span><span class="n">Stage 4</span><h3>Warehouse</h3><ul class="frict"><li>Put-away and picking delays</li><li>Stock located outside its recorded position</li><li>Allocation reassigned between orders</li><li>Counting interrupting outbound flow</li></ul></li><li class="stage"><span aria-hidden="true" class="dot"></span><span class="n">Stage 5</span><h3>Transport</h3><ul class="frict"><li>Carrier booking made late</li><li>Loads consolidated and split after booking</li><li>Collection windows missed</li><li>Status updates arriving after the event</li></ul></li><li class="stage"><span aria-hidden="true" class="dot"></span><span class="n">Stage 6</span><h3>Delivery and confirmation</h3><ul class="frict"><li>Proof of delivery captured late</li><li>Failed deliveries and redelivery</li><li>Short and damaged receipts</li><li>Returns opened at the point of delivery</li></ul></li></ol><div class="beneath"><h3>What runs underneath all six</h3><p>These four behaviours recur across the stages and often account for a large share of the cycle: work waiting in a queue without a clear owner, work looping back to a stage it already passed, work changing hands between teams with little record of the handoff, and the same delivery type running through materially different routes depending on who touched it.</p></div></div></div></section><section class="band method"><div class="shell band-grid"><div class="band-rail"><h2>How RE-ViVE observes it</h2></div><div class="band-body"><p class="intro">No process remodeling. No new workflow instrumentation. Read-only source access.</p><p class="method-note">You provide the relevant source records and limited subject-matter support. RE-ViVE does the reconstruction and the analysis.</p><ol class="steps"><li><span class="n">Step 1</span><h3>Read-only extracts</h3><p>Planning, order, production, warehouse and transport records from the systems that already hold them. Nothing is written back.</p></li><li><span class="n">Step 2</span><h3>Execution reconstructed</h3><p>RE-ViVE maps source records into its Execution Data Model, allowing each delivery to be reconstructed across the systems involved.</p></li><li><span class="n">Step 3</span><h3>Followed end to end</h3><p>Each delivery can then be followed from demand signal to delivery confirmed, with dwell time, loops and handoffs measured rather than estimated.</p></li><li><span class="n">Step 4</span><h3>Kept current</h3><p>The view refreshes as new deliveries are recorded, so the effect of any change is visible in the same measure.</p></li></ol></div></div></section><section class="band"><div class="shell band-grid"><div class="band-rail"><h2>What you are left holding</h2></div><div class="band-body"><p class="intro">Evidence specific enough to act on, and traceable back to the individual delivery behind it.</p><ul class="out"><li><h3>The routes deliveries actually take</h3><p>Every path present in the data, from demand signal to confirmed receipt, ranked by volume and by the effort it consumes.</p></li><li><h3>Where the days sit</h3><p>Lead time broken down by stage and split between movement, processing and waiting, so the delay has an address.</p></li><li><h3>Expediting and rework made countable</h3><p>Replans, reallocations, split shipments, premium freight and redeliveries, counted and costed rather than absorbed.</p></li><li><h3>Cause attributed</h3><p>Missed dates attributed by site, lane, supplier, carrier, product and customer, rather than to the team at the end of the chain.</p></li><li><h3>Promise reliability explained</h3><p>The point at which each commitment became undeliverable, separated from the point at which it was reported as missed.</p></li><li><h3>A monitored process</h3><p>Continuous visibility once the first view is built, giving a baseline that network and planning changes can be measured against.</p></li></ul></div></div></section><section class="band"><div class="shell band-grid"><div class="band-rail"><h2>Where the evidence lives</h2></div><div class="band-body"><p class="intro">Supply Chain and Fulfilment leaves a record in systems you already run.</p><ul class="sys-list"><li>ERP planning</li><li>Demand planning</li><li>Production and MRP</li><li>Warehouse management</li><li>Transport management</li><li>Supplier portals</li><li>Carrier and third-party logistics tracking</li><li>Quality management</li></ul><p class="sys-note">RE-ViVE works with the systems an enterprise already runs, including platforms such as SAP, Oracle, Blue Yonder and Manhattan, along with other planning, warehouse and transport platforms, supplier portals, carrier feeds, spreadsheets and custom applications. Where execution moves outside the available system evidence, RE-ViVE makes the unexplained interval visible for investigation.</p></div></div></section><section class="band"><div class="shell band-grid"><div class="band-rail"><h2>Processes next door</h2></div><div class="band-body"><p class="intro">Fulfilment shares its evidence with the processes it sits between.</p><ul class="next"><li class="f-fin"><a href="/process-order-to-cash"><span aria-hidden="true" class="lane"></span><div><h3>Order to Cash</h3><p>The commercial view of the same shipment, from the order that created it to the cash that settles it.</p></div><span class="go"><b>Open</b></span></a></li><li class="f-fin"><a href="/process-procure-to-pay"><span aria-hidden="true" class="lane"></span><div><h3>Procure to Pay</h3><p>The buying view of the supply stage, from requisition through receipt to supplier payment.</p></div><span class="go"><b>Open</b></span></a></li><li class="f-ops"><a href="/process-service-resolution"><span aria-hidden="true" class="lane"></span><div><h3>Service Request and Ticket Resolution</h3><p>Where failed deliveries and shortages arrive as customer requests to be resolved.</p></div><span class="go"><b>Open</b></span></a></li></ul></div></div></section><section class="close"><div class="shell"><h2 class="prose">See how your fulfilment actually executes</h2><p class="prose" style="margin-top:1.1rem;color:var(--ash);font-size:1.08rem">Give us read-only access to the planning and logistics data you already hold, and we will show you where the committed date is genuinely lost.</p><div class="cta"><a class="btn btn-line" href="/process">See all processes</a></div></div></section></main>
</Layout>
